# Spawning a role on each host

Read this in Step 3 when spawning an isolated subagent; skip it when the main session substitutes.

`devkit sync` renders role contracts into the selected native directories. If a projection is missing or stale,
repair it with `devkit status` and a normal `devkit sync` where authorized. Do not edit managed/projected files or
host trust settings by hand.

| Host | Mechanism | Projected file |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Claude Code | The subagent named `<role>` (Agent tool or `@agent-<role>`) | `.claude/agents/<role>.md` |
| Codex CLI | `collaboration.spawn_agent` with `agent_type: <role>` when available | `.codex/agents/<role>.toml` |
| Antigravity | `invoke_subagent` with the role name | `.agents/agents/<role>.md` |

## Availability and trust

- Prefer the projected named role and pass only the payload: the host loads its contract.
- A missing role can mean a missing or stale projection or a session started above the project root; role absence
  alone does not prove the root is untrusted. Diagnose availability separately from trust.
- If a session cannot load the named role, it MAY spawn a generic native subagent that reads and follows the
  effective managed/override contract by absolute path, only when host project-instruction loading is permitted.
  Pass that path in the payload and explicitly report emulation.
- Never replace an override with the upstream contract during fallback.

An explicit host trust restriction MUST stop this fallback. Do not use a generic role, another tool/process/path,
or host configuration edits to bypass it. If trust must change, the user controls that change. Do not diagnose or
promise trusted status from a successful unrelated operation.

Before starting a worker, establish that native spawning, required nested roles and the approval capability needed
for its actual operations are available.

- If one is unavailable, use existing delegation policy: the Host runs the unit in `direct` when allowed; `required`
  isolation stops with `needs_input`.
- Do not start a worker that cannot meet its required nested-role or approval obligations.
- Capability availability is not a promise that an approval will be granted. Starting a worker requires no
  persistent-grant preflight or separate probe framework.

## Codex worker access

Both workers are `emulated` with `sandbox_mode: workspace-write`. Native invocation supplies no native role/path
enforcement. Workers inherit model and reasoning effort; projected roles set no approval-policy override.
Normal devkit sibling worktrees and shared Git administration can lie outside active sandbox access.

- Set an absolute worktree cwd on every command, use `git -C <worktree>` for Git, and give nested-role read and
  contract paths as absolute paths.
- For each necessary out-of-sandbox operation, request a separate, narrowly scoped approval with the exact operation,
  target and justification: writes, shared Git, scope registry and verification/network access included.
- Use no broad persistent prefix. A successful preflight or approved call grants no later access.
- If approval is refused or unavailable, stop the operation; preserve and return its actual partial state under
  [worker](worker.md).

